    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 111752\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n1052 AM PDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* An incoming system will bring widespread precipitation chances\n  and an elevated flash flood risk to the area through the \n  weekend. \n\n* This system will also bring gusty winds to the region over the \n  weekend into early next week.\n\n* Cooler than normal temperatures will stick around through much\n  of next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through next Saturday.\n\nSunday looks to be the highest impact day, with showers and \nthunderstorms becoming more widespread overnight and continuing  \nthroughout the day on Sunday. Flash Flood Watches remain in effect \nfor most of the area through 5:00 AM PDT/MST on Monday. Evening \nsatellite imagery shows post-tropical cyclone Rachel drifting \ninland near Ensenada on the Baja Peninsula. Rachel's remnant \ncirculation will continue to track northeast, moving into southern\nSan Bernardino County early Sunday morning, moving up the \nColorado River Valley throughout the morning and early afternoon \nbefore moving into southwestern Utah by late afternoon. As Rachel \nswings through, showers and thunderstorms will become more \nwidespread through the morning and into the afternoon. Heavy rain,\nflash flooding, and strong winds will be the primary threats with\nthese storms. High resolution models show the potential for a \nswath of 1.0+ inch rain totals extending across the higher terrain\nof San Bernardino, into Death Valley, and southern Nevada by late\nmorning. In addition to heavy rains, flash flooding, and strong \nwinds, the Storm Prediction Center (SPC) has highlighted the lower\nColorado River Valley with a 2% tornado risk. Should we see a \ntornado develop, it will be short lived and on the lower end of \nthe EF-scale. As the remnants of Rachel begin to exit our forecast\narea, w will see an upper-level trough push into the area late \nSunday morning/early Sunday afternoon. This trough combined with \nthe lingering moisture from Rachel, will help to fuel another \nround of showers and thunderstorms across the region. This trough \nwill remain over the area as we go into next week, with chances \nfor isolated-to-scattered convection lingering through Tuesday \nbefore things dry out Wednesday onward.\n\nAs Rachel's remnant circulation approaches and moves through the \narea, we will see strong southerly winds pick up across the Mojave \nDesert with wind gusts in the 40 to 55 mph range. A Wind Advisory \nwill go into effect at 2:00 AM on Sunday for San Bernardino, \nClark, and Mohave Counties as Rachel begins to approach our \nforecast area. This Wind Advisory and the Wind Advisory for the \nsouthern Great Basin and Death Valley will remain in effect \nthrough 5:00 am Monday morning. As Rachel moves into up the \nColorado River Valley, we expect to see stronger winds mix down to\nthe surface, with 60+ mph wind gusts possible in the Colorado \nRiver Valley, Mohave County, southern and Northeastern Clark \nCounty, and Lincoln County. As such, a High Wind Warning will go \ninto effect for the aforementioned areas beginning at 5:00 am \nSunday morning and running through 8:00 pm Sunday evening. Breezy \nwinds will linger into early next week, but will not be as intense\nas winds on Sunday.\n\nThe aforementioned trough, which will fuel additional storms on \nSunday, will linger over the area through Tuesday, before exiting \nthe region on Wednesday and Thursday. As a result of these low 500 \nmb heights, temperatures will cool each day through Tuesday, \ngradually warming back up during the second half of the week as \n500 mb heights recover. Even with this late week warm up, \ntemperatures will remain cooler than normal as we head into next \nweekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 18Z Forecast\nPackage...Another challenging forecast period ahead, with \nperiodic showers expected through mid-evening, with a brief window\nlate afternoon through sunset when there is around a 20% chance \nof thunderstorms to develop. Confidence in the occurrence of \nthunderstorms is low to moderate, however, showers and storms will\nbe capable of producing brief moderate to heavy rain, resulting \nin ceilings down to around 3kft and visibility below 6SM. This \nevening, conditions will slowly improve as showers diminish and \nshift northeast of the Las Vegas Valley, though ceilings will \nremain SCT- BKN between 7-10kft. South-southwesterly wind gusts to\n25-35KT will continue through late evening outside of convective \ninfluences, with gusts diminishing thereafter. Mid-morning, have \nreintroduced VCSH for additional development, with a return of \ngusty southwesterly winds expected early afternoon. \n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 18Z Forecast Package...Unsettled weather\ncontinues across the region, with periodic showers and \nthunderstorms expected across southern Nevada, northwestern \nArizona, and portions of southeastern California through late \nevening. The greatest likelihood for isolated thunderstorms and \nheavier rain from showers exists across southern Nevada (KLAS, \nKVGT, KHND), northwestern Arizona, and the Lower Colorado River \nValley (KIFP and KEED), with showers expected to taper off from \nsouthwest to northeast this evening into tonight. Additional \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms will develop late tonight \nacross southern areas, and progress northward through Monday. \nCeilings in the vicinity of showers and storms could drop to 3kft \nor less, with improvement to 7-12kft expected tonight. \nElevated/gusty winds will continue, with gusts to 25-35KT common \nacross the region through this evening. Gusts diminish overnight, \nthen return for most locations near or just beyond the end of the \nforecast period.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Stessman\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
